TRANSIENT_BLOCK_SEG:
        ; Segment from INT 21h AH=48h; freed again with AH=49h at 0F00h
        ; How dangerous a stub label in transient code actually is.
        ;
        ; Adding 2020h to a stub address lands somewhere in the transient, and 189 of the
        ; stub labels have such a twin.  48 of them are named from transient code, so a
        ; reader has to decide which frame is meant 48 times.  The decision is easier than
        ; it looks, because of where the twins land:
        ;
        ; twin is a block START     0 of the 48   genuinely ambiguous
        ; twin is a block INTERIOR  48 of the 48  mid-string, so nonsense
        ;
        ; A memory operand like `[SHELL_FLAGS]` has 0339h+2020h = 2359h sitting in the
        ; middle of MSG_VOLUME_NA_UNIDADE, and no instruction loads the middle of a
        ; message.  That is why the stub reading is right at all 48.
        ;
        ; The exception is an immediate.  #1113 found two: 0621h and 0AA3h are
        ; FARCALL_SAVED_DX and SYSMSG_COMANDO_INVALIDO in the stub, and MSG_HORA_INVALIDA
        ; and MSG_ERRO_DE_ESCRITA_NO exactly -- block starts, not interiors -- in the
        ; transient.  Loading 621h into DX is equally plausible either way, and four sites
        ; were read the wrong way for a long time.  So the rule with teeth is not "stub or
        ; transient" but "does the twin start a block": if it does, the operand needs
        ; deciding on its own evidence rather than on which frame the code is in.
        ; Format: FORMAT_HEX
        ; raw
        db      0, 0                                           ;#0272

