FOR_VARIABLE_CHAR:
        ; The letter after '%'; 3F65h matches it to substitute the item
        ; Stub variables that transient code reaches directly.
        ;
        ; The transient runs in TRANSIENT_SEG but switches DS to LOW_COPY_SEG to touch
        ; the stub, so an address like 5F2h in transient code is a STUB address, not a
        ; transient one.  Transient code reaches 44 distinct stub addresses this way, and
        ; 36 of them land inside a declared message block if you wrongly add 2020h -- so a
        ; sweep looking for code that patches message text reports all 36 as false
        ; positives.  The giveaway is `test byte [SHELL_FLAGS], SHELL_FOR_ACTIVE` at
        ; FOR_NEXT_ITEM, which only resolves because SHELL_FLAGS is a stub label.
        ;
        ; Deliberately no list.  This note used to enumerate eighteen of them, and #1027
        ; read that as coverage and tripped on one that was not there; the set grows every
        ; time another stub variable is named, so the count is a measurement and the rule
        ; is the thing to carry: in transient code, DS decides, and DS is LOW_COPY_SEG
        ; wherever the routine loaded it from there.
        ;
        ; Seven blocks really are patched before printing, and stub code does all of it:
        ; AUTOEXEC_PATH, ERRMSG_AREA_AFETADA, ERRMSG_NA_UNIDADE, ENV_NAME_COMSPEC_1,
        ; MSG_SISNE_PLUS, SYSMSG_COLOQUE_O_COMMAND_COM_NA_UNIDA and SYSMSG_ERRO_DE.
        ; That follows from where the tables live: all of them are below 2120h.
        ; Format: FORMAT_HEX
        ; raw
        db      0                                              ;#05EF

