SCREEN_IS_MONO:
        ; Set when INT 10h AH=0Fh reports mode 7
        ; The screen script's variables and FOR's DTA are the same bytes.
        ;
        ; FOR_DTA is 05F6h and a find-result DTA is 43 bytes, so it runs to 0620h --
        ; exactly where FARCALL_SAVED_DX begins, which is the corroboration that the
        ; length is right.  Laid against the DTA's layout, everything from 0611h on is
        ; a second use of a field DOS fills:
        ;
        ; 0603h  FOR_DTA_ENTRY_NUM  +0Dh  entry index, tested against 0FFFFh
        ; 0611h  SCREEN_IS_MONO     +1Bh  inside DTA_SIZE_LO
        ; 0612h  SCREEN_PAGE        +1Ch  DTA_SIZE_HI
        ; 0614h  SCRIPT_COL         +1Eh  DTA_NAME, first character
        ; 061Bh  SCREEN_VARS_TAIL   +25h  DTA_NAME, last six bytes
        ;
        ; 3F18h proves the name is read there: `mov si, FOR_DTA` then IS_DOT_ENTRY,
        ; which looks at [si+DTA_NAME].  The overlay is safe because the sign-on runs
        ; once before any command is typed and FOR cannot run until after, so nothing
        ; needs both meanings at once.  It also explains why #1062 called 061Bh..0620h
